Build depends:
  GCC or Clang compiler
  SQLite version 3.30.1 or newer
  libxml2 development headers and pkg-config
  Qt 5.15.x (development headers required)
  QtSingleApplication built for Qt 5, including headers, library and
    qtsingleapplication.prf (Fedora: qtsingleapplication-qt5-devel)
  Required Qt development tools:
    qmake
    lrelease
  Required Qt libraries:
    QtCore
    QtGui
    QtWidgets
    QtPrintSupport
    QtMultimedia
    QtNetwork
    QtSql compiled with sqlite support
    QtXml

Runtime image support:
  Full article image support requires Qt image-format plugins, including WebP.
  On Fedora, install qt5-qtimageformats; packaged builds should depend on it at
  runtime. This is not a compiler or development-header dependency.
  Bundled distributions must deploy the matching Qt image-format plugins.
  Without WebP support, the reader does not advertise WebP to image servers,
  but images available only in WebP format still cannot be displayed.

Instructions for Linux and other UNIX-like systems:
  mkdir _build && cd _build
  qmake CONFIG+=release PREFIX=/usr ../QuiteRSS.pro
  make -j3
  make install

Instead of -j3 make option you can use -j<number of your CPU cores + 1>.
For making debug build use CONFIG+=debug instead of CONFIG+=release.
By default qmake uses gcc, for using clang you need to alter qmake command:
  qmake QMAKE_CC=clang QMAKE_CXX=clang++ QMAKE_LINK=clang++ QMAKE_LINK_C=clang CONFIG+=release PREFIX=/usr ../QuiteRSS.pro

QtSingleApplication must be installed before running qmake on every platform.
The bundled copy is no longer used; SYSTEMQTSA is no longer needed.
On Fedora: sudo dnf install qtsingleapplication-qt5-devel
The Qt 6 package cannot be used with this Qt 5 application.
For a custom dependency installation, put its qtsingleapplication.prf directory
on QMAKEFEATURES. The feature must set the installed include and library paths.
Use a library built with a compatible Qt version, compiler and architecture.
Distributors must also provide the QtSingleApplication shared library at runtime
when linking dynamically.

Instructions for Windows:
  Use a Qt 5.15 toolchain.
  From the compiler environment, run:
    qmake CONFIG+=release CONFIG-=debug_and_release QuiteRSS.pro
    nmake
  For MinGW, use mingw32-make instead of nmake.

Instructions for Mac OS:
  cd /path/to/build
  qmake CONFIG+=release /path/to/quiterss/QuiteRSS.pro
  make -j3
  make install
  cd /path/to/build/release/target
  macdeployqt quiterss.app/ -dmg

Instead of -j3 make option you can use -j<number of your CPU cores + 1>.
